    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121179",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "In a school, a survey conducted on the $40$ students shows that $17$ students traveled to school by train, $23$ students traveled by bus, and $10$ students traveled by both train and bus. \n\nIf $T$ represents the event that a student travels by train, and $B$ represents the event that a student travels by bus, which of the following diagrams gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?",
      "worked_solution": "Note that there are $10$ students who traveled by both train and bus. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $T$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who traveled by train *only*  is\n\n$$\n17-10=7,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $7$ in the part of circle $T$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who traveled by bus *only*  is\n\n$$\n23-10=13,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $13$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $T.$\n\nThere are $40$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n40-7-10-13=10.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $10$ remaining students, we write the number $10$ outside of the circles $T$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/a511835f1ecf6a7f3991f8ebb9ed14339ebfab4cb41aa06363502df5abf03719.png)",
